WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.920
The number most often quoted for hair cells is 40 microseconds.

2
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.780
Is that how long one channel takes to open?

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.260
It is not. It is a corrected delay, measured across a whole piece of tissue.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.740
Direct channel recordings are also very fast.

5
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.480
So when someone says speed, which quantity did they measure?

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.820
Corey and Hudspeth stimulated hair bundles in an in vitro bullfrog sacculus.

7
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.840
They recorded the summed electrical response across the sheet of cells.

8
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.080
The electrical time constant of the epithelium slows that signal.

9
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.920
After correcting for it, the delay was 40 microseconds at 22 degrees Celsius.

10
00:00:41.783 --> 00:00:44.503
Four different quantities travel under the word speed.

11
00:00:45.323 --> 00:00:48.603
A tissue latency is the delay of a population of cells.

12
00:00:49.323 --> 00:00:53.183
A channel time constant is how fast a current rises after a step.

13
00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:56.983
A molecular gain counts output events per input event.

14
00:00:57.603 --> 00:01:02.423
A behavioural latency runs from stimulus to movement, and no channel time constant

15
00:01:02.423 --> 00:01:03.523
stands in for it.

16
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.767
Now watch a channel time constant at work.

17
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.367
Suppose a taste cell depolarizes for 3 milliseconds.

18
00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:18.507
In cultured mouse N2a cells, CALHM1 alone activated with a time constant above 500

19
00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:24.307
milliseconds. With CALHM3 added, the time constant was about 10 milliseconds.

20
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.100
Model a channel that starts closed and rises as a single exponential.

21
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:36.320
With a time constant of 10 milliseconds, 3 milliseconds reaches about 26% % of the

22
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:43.120
maximum. With 500 it reaches about 0 .6%, and in this model it is smaller still.

23
00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:47.460
So the partner makes the channel about 43 times more responsive.

24
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.100
These are teaching assumptions, not measured release.

25
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:54.383
A fast latency can fit a candidate channel.

26
00:01:54.923 --> 00:01:57.383
It is a constraint on the kinetics, not a proof.

27
00:01:58.143 --> 00:02:00.223
Speed alone does not identify the channel.

28
00:02:00.763 --> 00:02:03.043
The 40 microseconds describes a tissue.

29
00:02:03.563 --> 00:02:06.723
The 10 milliseconds describes a channel in cultured cells.

30
00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:10.963
Before you trust a speed, ask which quantity it is, and in which cell.
